From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:43:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] download: fail a post-message that is gone from the
 server
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A download-on-demand message whose post-message the server no longer has
spun forever instead of failing. `download_msg()` looks the message's IMAP
location up, and when there is none left it also deleted the message from
`available_post_msgs` before returning the error — which is the very flag
`download_msgs()` then uses to decide whether the failure is final. With
the flag gone it took the "will be downloaded once it arrives" branch,
kept the `download` row and retried on every fetch cycle, so the bubble
showed "downloading" with no percentage, no failure and no retry button,
for good. A chatmail relay deleting the message after a while is enough to
reach this.

The decision is now made where the flag is still intact: a message that
was announced as available and has no location left is set to `Failure`
and dropped from the queue, which is what puts the retry button back. A
post-message that has merely not arrived yet was never announced, so it
keeps waiting exactly as before — the flag and the location row are
written by the same prefetch pass, so "announced" really does mean "was on
the server".

The location lookup is now `imap_location()`, named because its
transport-spanning behavior is load-bearing: relays expire messages on
their own schedules, so only a message no transport has a location for may
be failed, and one another relay still holds stays that relay's job. Its
test pins all four cases.

A relay that proves it does not serve the UID anymore (the chunked path's
re-probe finds nothing) now also has its `imap` row deleted, so the retry
looks the message up afresh instead of asking the same dead relay again,
and the last location leaving is what makes the message failable. The
whole-message fallback cannot tell "gone" from "fetch failed" and leaves
its row alone.

Also fixes the chunked path leaving the same dead `InProgress` behind when
`receive_imf` rejects the assembled message: the device message was posted
and the queue entry dropped, but nothing moved the message out of
`InProgress`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
